> across Ultrix/OSF platforms and would it make any difference if I
> moved the mail filesystem from Ultrix to OSF?

As far as I can tell, NFS file locking has never worked reliably, on
any platform.

However, we have been using NFS-mounted mailboxes for several years
without too many problems.  The key is to always have mail routed to
the mail server machine (which has the mailboxes on local disk) for
delivery.  This can be done by tweaking sendmail config files on both
the client machines (to send all mail to the server) and the server
machine (to accept mail for all client domains for local delivery).

There are other tricks which also help, including using an automounter
on the mail server to mount people's "home directories", and a special
delivery agent that interprets a missing "home directory" as a
termporary failure condition (so if the user has a .forward file and
the file system is down, the user's mail will get queued until the
.forward file is available again.)

To eliminiate quirks of various vendor-supplied sendmails, we use
Berkeley sendmail on all of our platforms (which include several
different UNIX variants).  With Berkeley sendmail, our OSF/1 clients
have worked acceptably with such a configuration.  We have not tried
doing this with DEC sendmail, nor using an OSF/1 machine as a mail
server.

