>you can get your mail anywhere, and people don't have to know what
>node you work on to send you mail.  And it's a lot easier than having
>to worry about a central mail hub.

Mail hubs are pretty easy to set up, and they are a lot more reliable
than rpc lockd.

>The problem is that we're adding OSF/1 workstations, and they aren't
>being very cooperative.  It's no problem to mount the mail filesystem
>and read messages from it, but it you try to mail anything to it you
>get various kinds of locking problems.

Par for the course.  lockd is almost always unreliable in a heterogeneous
environment.

>  I can find very little doc on
>file locking with mail under Ultrix.

Well since Unix vendors can't figure out how to implement lockd
in a functional way, it's no surprise that they can't document it well
either.

>  I'm beginning to suspect that
>this was tightened up on OSF to get rid of some mail related security
>problems. The question is whether this basic strategy will work at all
>across Ultrix/OSF platforms and would it make any difference if I
>moved the mail filesystem from Ultrix to OSF?

Doubtful, but anything's possible.

I suggest you pick up POP and IMAP, Berkeley sendmail v8, MX all
your hosts to the hub and have client hosts forward everything to
the hub for delivery (local and remote delivery).  It's a much more
effective solution for scaling purposes.


