In article <3c1qv5$d8f@bock.freinet.de>, wolpert@kandel.ds.freinet.de (Florian Wolpert) says:
>
>Hi,
>
>I'm looking for an integration of E-Mail (RFC-822) on a PC-Network
>running pathworks. I want to use an OSF/1 machine as mailhub and gateway
>to the Internet.
>
>Any hints ?
>
>- Flo.
>--
>          XLINK-POP in Freiburg, nicht fragen - anschliessen!

PATHWORKS includes a basic (character cell) mail client that can be run
from Windows with a .PIF.  Before running the client, you need to set
it up with the MAILSETU utility.

The PATHWORKS server on OSF/1 (as well as those on ULTRIX, OpenVMS and
OS/2) includes a mail server that talks to the mail clients on the front
end and to SMTP and Digital Mail-11 protocol (used by VMSmail, etc.)
on the back end.  Mail-11 talks to the Digital Message Router, and via
it to X.400, ALL-IN-1, SNADS, PROFS, etc.

If you want a more full-function mail product, Digital has a product
family called DEC MailWorks which is a full X.400 implementation, but
that also talks to SMTP.  There are clients for Windows, Macintosh,
Motif and terminals.  MailWorks can also be used via MAPI-compatible
service providers with popular mail clients, such as MS Mail and cc:
Mail.  DEC MailWorks is sold separately, but obviously runs over
PATHWORKS.



