In article <3d486a$23k@amhux3.amherst.edu>, pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:
>
>We're about to augment our DEC Ultrix machine with a DECStation
>running OSF/1.  It's fairly certain that some of my users are going to
>ask why we're doing this, and in particular, what's in it for them
>that's sufficiently important that they have to learn a new Unix
>varient.

Will they?  Trying to put on a pure-user hat of an OSF/1 system,
I can think of very few differences from ULTRIX.  When I can
think of differences they fall into things that only the system
administrator *should* have to worry about.  There are also
some programming differences.

Programming:

o  C longs are now 64 bits as are pointers.  This may break
   poorly written programs that made bad assumptions about
   sizes and alignments of things.

o  OSF has shared libraries and memory mapping of files.  ULTRIX
   didn't.  These considered features most places.

Systems programming:

o  For programs that go looking at kernel data structures, the
   OSF kernel is very different.  Many of the things that were
   only avaialble by reading /dev/kmem on ULTRIX are now available
   via the table(2) and getsysinfo(2) system calls or through the
   procfs interface.

Other differences:

o  What kind of MIPS DECsystem/station do you have?  What kind
   of Alpha system?  For CPU bound processes what's the difference
   in speed between the two?

o  ULTRIX had a 2 GB file size limit as well as 2 GB file system
   size limit.  UFS on OSF is limited to only 32 GB, but the
   Advanced File System has been tested up to 128 GB.

System adminstration.  Probably the most visible differences
are here:

o  Replacement of /etc/rc.local with a suite of scripts for different
   "run levels".

o  Replacement of /etc/ttys with /etc/inittab.

o  No pstat.  Unfortunately there also hasn't been a good all
   purpose replacement.

o  There's been some detailed rearrangement of files within the
   file system, but many of the ages old locations of things
   have been preserved through the use of symbolic links.

I'd guess that there is less different between OSF and ULTRIX
than there was between SunOS 4.x and Solaris 2.x.

Without knowing what sort of environment your typical user
expects it is really hard to guess that they'll see that's
different.  If an a user ever does is ls, page, vi, make,
and run the application, they may not notice any difference
except the speed difference.  Such a person may notice some
include files have moved slightly, but that's often the
price of writing portable code.
 
