Don.Wilkerson@gjpomail.doegjpo.com (Don Wilkerson) writes:

>I'm thinking of setting up a DEC 3000/600 Axp with Web, Gopher, News, etc... 
>and I was looking for opinions on how well an Axp with OSF works as compared 
>to other systems.  Concerns would be ease of installation as compared to other 
>systems.  Would OSF on the Alpha be a good choice for an Internet server or 
>would you suggest another system setup.

I believe wuarchive.wustl.edu is a OSF/1 system and has been since the
first release of OSF/1.  (I say "I believe" because I haven't heard of
any NFS problems on the archive machine since those early days.

Ah - must be, as they support NFS V3:

% rpcinfo -p wuarchive.wustl.edu
   program vers proto   port
    100000    2   tcp    111  portmapper
    100000    2   udp    111  portmapper
    100003    2   udp   2049  nfs
    100003    3   udp   2049  nfs
    100005    1   udp   4919  mountd
    100005    3   udp   4919  mountd
    100005    1   tcp    711  mountd
    100005    3   tcp    711  mountd

[BTW - does anyone have experience with using wustl via NFS V3?  Our
 firewalls and odd routing make it difficult for me to experiment much.
 V3 has some nice features that should cut access times, especially
 for things like "ls -l".]

Also, they seem to be exporting only one directory for their entire archive.
I think they're using AdvFS, which allows adding new volumes to a file
systems without having to rebuild the thing.  I know some customers have
taken advantage of that for USENET, which just keeps growing and growing
and growing....

The WWW California Election Results are served by three 2100
servers running OSF/1.  See
http://www.election.digital.com/e/misc/info.servers.html for details.
I think the folks who put it together ran into one problem with the
high connection rate, but that was quickly fixed.  For less taxing WWW
serving, I doubt you'll have trouble!

As for ease of installation, I doubt Digital offers a WWW installation
package, but as for installing from scratch, it's pretty easy.  Maintaining
and upgrading are easy.
