In article <3cpf4n$r7n@crl10.crl.com>, cgi@crl.com (Paul Smith) writes:
> With the notable and for me a cause for some re-work, OSF/1's protocol stacks
> are BSD style, linked into the kernel, as apposed to SYSV and Spec1170 infered
> of being streams based.  I.E. socket() does NOT return a stream based fd.
> Nor is OSF's X.25 stack streams based.  Not being streams based is an
> issue for the OSI plug and play (yuk, yuk) streams based interfaces.
> 
> For me, it meant that you can't poll() a socket, only select().

Two things:

First, while it is true that socket() does not return a Streams fd, you
can get Streams access to protocols through the TLI and XTI interfaces,
which provide standard API's and Streams interfaces.

Second, OSF/1 can poll() or select() any file descriptor, i.e. it does not
suffer from the System V restriction that poll() only works on Streams.

Tom Talpey
