Get real! Give me physical access to a computer and I can break in. It's as
simple as that. Any good sysadmin class on any OS covers just how to do it as
should the system documentation. IF you expect a computer to be unbreakable
with physical access, you are living in a dream world. Building such a computer
would be a true disaster for the manufacturer. It is, as Digital called it, a
feature. It is present in avery multi-user OS I have ever worked with from
every manufacturer. And in many cases, it was clearly implemented very
deliberately with extra system variables and hooks to make it work.

You may not like this fact, but it is a fact and it's not likely to ever
change. Sysadmins would simply not accept it. I surly would not and I have
never lost a root password! But I have often had to break into abandoned
systems where no one knows the password for root or any other account.

If this is a problem, and in a student environment it might well be, look into
MIT Athena. While no workstation is secure, the system is very secure and there
has never been a break-in that didn't involve a lost/stolen password. It was
designed as it was for the simple reason that any time a person has physical
access to a system, he can control it completely.

Now that you have called everyone else stupid, I guess you should go off and
invent your own way of securing a system. I might suggest putting it in a
vault. That's what the government does. Not very usable, but it's the only way
I know of to keep a system truly secure.

