>I'm just thinking about upgrading our machines from OSF v2.1 to v3.0.
>There are some nice things in v3 like NFS v3 (with 64 bit file access
>and asynchronous writes) and dynamic kernel configuration.
>
>Is upgrading from v2.1 to v3.0 as traumatic as going from v1.3 to v2.0?
>i.e. will I need to recompile virtually everything!

I had to recompile a few applications (top and gcc), but most of the
applications worked without problems. It was actually rather easy to
guess which applications that would need recompilation.

>Also, have DEC done anything to improve the memory usage by OSF, or
>is v3 just as much of a memory pig as v2?

v3 seems to use less memory than v2, but it still uses far more memory
than for example SunOS 4.1.x (but on the other hand most OS are
serious memory hogs if you compare them to SunOS 4, including Solaris 2)

I have only tested 3.0 on my own machine (3000/300LX with 96 MB), so
it's a bit hard to quantify how much less memory it uses. It is also a
question of tuning, someone with a 32 MB machine said that he had
managed to reduce the paging a bit by changing some parameters with
sysconfigtab.


