This is brilliant logic but not upheld by reality.  OSF/1 is vastly bigger
than Ultrix both in terms of memory and disk space requirements.  I 
routinely fill a 1Gb disk with the operating system, a healthy swap partition,
and a basic set of end-user freeware & GNU tools, without having room
left over for source code and home directories.  When we moved our main staff
machine from a SPARCstation2 to an Alpha, I ended up adding another 2Gb of
disk and doubling memory -- the binaries are a LOT bigger -- this for
a machine with only 10-15 users.

ULTRIX log.us.itd.umich.edu 4.2 0 RISC
-rwxr-xr-x  1 root      2761504 Nov 15  1991 /vmunix.gen*

OSF1 notorious.hq.cic.net V2.0 240 alpha
-rwxr-xr-x   1 root     system   9716136 Dec  5 15:50 /genvmunix*

Just the kernel is bigger by a factor of more than three.  Additionally,
all the major applications are bigger, and there are more of them.

>The availability of a driver under OSF/1 for the DEC864 graphics card is a
>complete unknown.

It's conceivable that the integration of XFree86 into the R6 source tree
has resulted in enough code to do what you want.  I'm fairly certain that
there is an S3 864 driver in the latest XFree86 release, and I believe that
X11R6 has been mostly cleaned for use with the Alpha except for not having
drivers for the Alpha hardware (DEC -- GIVE UP THE DAMN SPECS ALREADY! Grrr..)

So, uh, go for it ;)

