In article <3catvu$9fp@newsbf01.news.aol.com>,
WAYMEISTER <waymeister@aol.com> wrote:
>I am interested to hear from anyone that has experience on both operating
>systems.  We are installing SAP and currently exploring both possibilities
>(Alpha,  HP server).  My understanding is that the Alpha will run both
>OSF/1 and Windows NT.  How does OSF/1 stack up to HP-UX?

I really don't want to start a flame-war & I want to preface this by
saying I've got much more experience with BSD based systems than SYSV
systems, so my views are biased in favor of OSF/1 from the start.  

The way I see it, OSF/1 has a number of advantages over HP-UX:

-NFS3 This is the new standard in NFS.  DEC authored the standard
(with Sun), and have had it in the field since August.  It allows
*very* fast NFS writes (we see > 800k/sec NFS *writes* over 10BT
wires).

-ADVFS This is a modern file-system.  It permits mirroring & striping
of filesystems across disks & controllers.  This enhances performance
& allows you to do a safe backup while the system is running, and to
add & remove partitions from a filesystem while the machine is up.  In
addition, its a logging filesystem, which means no more waiting 30
minutes for your server to fsck its disks after a crash.  By contrast,
HPUX doesn't even allow you to partition the disk where root is
located.

-Its BSD-based.  This means that most things are where they 'should
be' (to a person who comes from a BSD backround), and work the way
they 'should work' (again, to a person from a BSD background).  

I see the advantages of HP-UX as follows:

-Its got a larger market share, so there's more commercial software
 available

-Its slighly easier to administer if you know nothing about Unix.  Its
got this graphical tool called SAM that you can use to configure the
kernel, add users, etc.  I find it quite annoying, but if you're not a
unix admin & don't want to learn, you might like it.

-If you like SYSV, then you'll probably like it better.

Good luck,

Drew

