In article <D0Exxo.8sv@clnsnet.ariadne-t.gr>, kassapidis@thission.iisr.noa.ariadne-t.gr (Panayotis Kassapidis, IISR/NOA) writes:

>
> [ Previous problem description deleted.   Summary, somebody
>   did a rm * in / and all the non-directory files went away. ]

> I chose to try the second way. I copied osf_boot and vmunix from another
>system (physically quite far from ours) to a nearby DECstation. My question
>is, is it possible to mount an OSF/1 V1.3 filesystem under Ultrix V4.2?

It probably won't hurt anything to try it, but some of the
likely problems are:

1.  ULTRIX won't have a clue what to do with the OSF disklabel.

2.  Since OSF probably didn't write a partition table at the
    end of the superblock, ULTRIX will rely on the its default
    which is probably wrong.

The result of #1 and #2 is that unless your OSF file system just
happened to be the same size as the ULTRIX default, you probably
won't be able to see all the root.  You might try the C partition
though, since it usually covers the whole disk and the superblock
will limit the useful size of the partition.

Then run fsck(8).  Don't let it repair anything until you know
what it will try to fix.  If too many things are "broken", then
it may not be possible to move from OSF back to ULTRIX.  (ULTRIX
to OSF) is supported.

If the repairs that fsck wants to do are nothing more than fixing
up the cylinder group summaries, then you should be ok.

