Message-ID: <3c1pbf$nl7@jac.zko.dec.com>
References: <3c0fek$jjs@narnia.ccs.neu.edu>
Reply-To: lueck@zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
NNTP-Posting-Host: gurgi.zk3.dec.com
X-Newsreader: mxrn 6.18-5


Your program is not showing a virtual memory bug, but is exceeding the
process's default stack size limit.  By default, a process can have only
2M of stack.  If a process exceeds this limit, the kernel reports an access
violation for the memory reference that causes the stack to grow too big.

You can change the stack size limit from the shell by doing this:

From the csh:

	% limit stacksize unlimited

From the bourne or korn shells:

	$ ulimit -s 32768

You can also change the limit from within the program by using this code
fragment near the beginning:

	#include <sys/resource.h>

	struct rlimit	rlim;

	getrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK, &rlim);
	rlim.rlim_cur = rlim.rlim_max;
	setrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK, &rlim);

