After upgrading all our AXPs from V2.0 to V3.0 of OSF/1, I discovered that
there are some really fundamental problems with tar (or actually, with the
new "pax" command, which "tar" is just a link to).  Here's just one example
(others are documented in separate messages) ...

Under OSF/1 V3, tar creates archives which can be read by OSF/1's tar, but
which other flavours of tar (e.g. IRIX4, SunOS4) have some problems with.
Specifically, the information in the archive that indicates something is a
directory isn't recognized by the other vendors' versions of tar.  (For all
I know, OSF/1 V3 tar might not be compatible with OSF/1 V2 tar, either.)

Starting with the following hierarchy ...

	foo/foo1/foo1.a
	foo/foo1/foo1.b
	foo/foo2/foo2.a
	foo/foo2/foo2.b
	bar/bar1/bar1.a
	bar/bar1/bar1.b
	bar/bar2/bar2.a
	bar/bar2/bar2.b

... I created three tar archives (one each under OSF/1, IRIX, and SunOS), in
the same way ("tar cf spam.`uname` foo bar").  Note that OSF/1 has no problem
reading the IRIX and SunOS versions:

moose> for x in spam.*; do
moose>> echo ===== $x =====
moose>> tar tvf $x
moose>> done
