> 	tmp.bytes[2] = bytes[2] ;
> 	tmp.bytes[3] = bytes[3] ;
> 
> 	return tmp.word ;
> }
> 

You can also use the following construct:

#pragma member_alignment save
#pragma nomember_alignment
struct {
   [definitions for struct you want no padding for]
};
#pragma member_alignment restore

if you use the -migrate option on the C compiler. This gives you
access to the DEC C compiler (you may need to have the
CMPDEVENH200 subset installed). Check out the cc(1) man page for
more information on the -migrate switch.

The compiler will then generate the appropriate instruction sequences
so that unaligned accessed do not occur. The advantage of this
approach is that you can control the padding on a structure by
structure basis.

Hope this helps,

Tim.



