Tom Kraljevic <tomk@dual.crhc.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>nodog@sabine.psu.edu (Anderson Mills) writes:
>>swap space below 10% free
>
>I had this same problem.  What you are probably suffering from
>is an overgrown xdm process.  Try adding the following line to
>/usr/lib/X11/xdm/xdm-config:
>
>DisplayManager._0.terminateServer:      true

While it's true that X is a leaky pig on OSF/1, this swap allocation problem
more likely results from OSF/1's method of allocating swap space (that is,
very liberal allocation, much more than a process needs when it starts).
In fact, it's likely that when you get the 10% message, you have almost all
your swap *reserved* but almost none of it in use.

rm -f /sbin/swapdefault 

will change the allocation method.  you'll get a boot-time warning that
says you're swapping in "overcommitment" mode.  I liken this to overbooking
airline seats -- you can almost always get away with it.  Then when things
go wrong you have to look for "volunteers" to get killed ;)  I haven't
been burned yet; I use 128mb swap on a 64mb 3000-300LX with about
20 users on it running pine, news, mosaic, a couple x terminals and the
odd compile job.  Running OSF/1 2.0.

--Paul



